1
00:00:01,890 --> 00:00:09,070
 We've changed our planet

2
00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:14,130
 These events have made you the man that you are

3
00:00:14,130 --> 00:00:16,900
 They've all imagined

4
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:18,080
 All found

5
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:21,030
 The moon that gravitates

6
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:22,930
 And the sea that falls off

7
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:28,440
 They've been trying to measure the time that gives them

8
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:32,810
 They've offered you all the gifts of happiness

9
00:00:32,810 --> 00:00:41,800
 A path to the dreams of humans

10
00:00:41,800 --> 00:01:06,620
 Follow your journey to other chimelles

11
00:01:06,620 --> 00:02:14,350
 We're going for the vein in the neck. This one here.

12
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:19,370
 The jugular vein. I guess it's where the body's most vulnerable.

13
00:02:19,590 --> 00:02:23,250
 Children, if I were you, I wouldn't be too worried. Dogs almost never kill each other.

14
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:24,890
 No wolves come to that.

15
00:02:25,330 --> 00:02:26,730
 With men, it's different.

16
00:02:27,090 --> 00:02:28,670
 Yes, but man's not an animal.

17
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:30,490
 Man's an animal, all right.

18
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:33,100
 Do I look like some kind of animal? Do I?

19
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:36,530
 Yeah. To me, you look like a wild animal.

20
00:02:36,530 --> 00:02:49,700
 I'm saying we humans are animals. I admit we're different, but they're resemblances as well.

21
00:02:50,170 --> 00:02:51,930
 Different but the same. How's that possible?

22
00:02:52,290 --> 00:02:55,930
 Obviously, we have hearts, a nervous system, reflexes, instincts, same as animals.

23
00:02:56,170 --> 00:02:58,490
 And animals are not machines, as was once supposed.

24
00:02:58,790 --> 00:03:00,370
 Here's what the great Descartes said.

25
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:06,130
 Animal, it's no more than a machine with its cogs and its springs.

26
00:03:06,270 --> 00:03:07,910
 We know exactly what it will do.

27
00:03:08,210 --> 00:03:11,350
 Should I pull its tail, it turns its head and barks to clear machine.

28
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:17,150
 Should I pat its head with my hand, it will wag its tail.

29
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:22,380
 It is evidently a spring-like thing linking head and tail.

30
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:25,530
 Pavlov, a Russian scientist, thought animals reacted by reflex.

31
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:26,730
 It is dinner time.

32
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:27,490
 Heard the bell.

33
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:34,850
 Ah, he's accustomed to having dinner when I ring this.

34
00:03:34,990 --> 00:03:38,470
 And so all I have to do is ring this little bell and it rules by reflex.

35
00:03:38,550 --> 00:03:39,870
 It is simple. Couldn't be simpler.

36
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:42,180
 No, the same. It's not as simple as all that.

37
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:45,300
 Animals are creatures. They're complex with nervous systems.

38
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:46,700
 Some are very intelligent.

39
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:51,760
 To understand them, we have to study their behavior in their environment in nature.

40
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:53,120
 And that's called ethology.

41
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:55,080
 Here we go with the Greeks.

42
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,680
 Not at all. For once, it won't be the Greeks.

43
00:03:58,270 --> 00:03:59,770
 Ethology is a modern science.

44
00:03:59,990 --> 00:04:03,750
 A question, Maestro? Who was it that figured out the thigh logic?

45
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:15,110
 Now, let me see, let me see. There was, well, there was, uh, besides, uh, anyway, the individual who best comprehended animals was surely the Austrian Conrad Lorenz.

46
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:26,930
 Gloria, you're a big nuisance. Impossible to let you be out when I'm working. Go on now, back in your cage. Go on now.

47
00:04:33,960 --> 00:05:52,810
 See you later, cage. Mr. Blone, I'll change it.

48
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:13,050
 Goodness, you do want to listen, but I'll admit you've taught me something.

49
00:06:13,410 --> 00:06:16,190
 Oh, you should, listen, I...

50
00:06:16,190 --> 00:06:20,540
 Many inquisitive active animals cannot bear to be in captivity.

51
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:23,400
 We have to study these creatures and their natural surroundings.

52
00:06:29,710 --> 00:06:45,680
 Since you're pregnant, that's not like me.

53
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:47,230
 I don't know, I'm not sure, Father.

54
00:06:47,490 --> 00:06:49,330
 I'd also like to study animals.

55
00:06:49,510 --> 00:06:50,650
 Oh, you'll be pleased then.

56
00:06:50,690 --> 00:06:53,310
 The neighbor's brought back a cockatoo, but it's very sad.

57
00:06:53,390 --> 00:06:53,990
 Kept in a cage?

58
00:06:54,070 --> 00:06:54,390
 Of course.

59
00:06:54,490 --> 00:06:55,210
 That's why it's sad.

60
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:05,060
 Oh, Conrad, as if you have enough already.

61
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:06,520
 What will you do with it?

62
00:07:06,930 --> 00:07:09,720
 Well, to begin with, I'll release it, and then we'll see.

63
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:33,440
 But no, thanks, I'm not hungry.

64
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:57,500
 This, of many animals, is similar in many ways to man's. This is clearly observed.

65
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:09,140
 He's got gosh.

66
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:14,820
 Guy, big and strong.

67
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:15,820
 Wonder if he's with anyone.

68
00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:23,370
 Show him.

69
00:08:23,370 --> 00:08:30,030
 He got pretty eyes, sweetheart.

70
00:08:30,030 --> 00:09:11,440
 Do you like him?

71
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:15,870
 You see, Margaret, when jackdaws marry, it's for life, and they always stay faithful.

72
00:09:15,870 --> 00:09:17,110
 Just like humans.

73
00:09:17,110 --> 00:09:19,110
 Just as you say, Margaret, yes, just as you say.

74
00:09:19,110 --> 00:09:26,700
 Do we ought to think about making a nest?

75
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:28,710
 Sure, sweetheart.

76
00:09:28,710 --> 00:09:39,620
 We'll be fine here.

77
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:41,620
 This is my place. It's reserved.

78
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:44,380
 No, it's mine. Watch it. I'm bigger than you and stronger.

79
00:09:44,580 --> 00:09:47,560
 Oh, you think so? Well, we'll just see about that.

80
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:56,290
 Pair each other to pieces. Separate them.

81
00:09:56,970 --> 00:09:59,330
 No, Margaret. They won't even hurt each other.

82
00:10:00,130 --> 00:10:02,650
 You'll see. We'll have plenty of time to pick strawberries.

83
00:10:05,510 --> 00:10:09,850
 You see? They're still here, but they're now really fatigued.

84
00:10:09,990 --> 00:10:10,510
 Wait, you'll see.

85
00:10:11,410 --> 00:10:13,450
 This place, I'll find a better one.

86
00:10:13,830 --> 00:10:16,970
 I won. I won because I was bigger and stronger.

87
00:10:17,330 --> 00:10:17,550
 Whoa.

88
00:10:18,850 --> 00:10:21,580
 You see? And humans behave in the same way.

89
00:10:21,580 --> 00:10:24,060
 We would frighten enemies, but we don't want to fight.

90
00:10:24,220 --> 00:10:25,470
 We might be injured.

91
00:10:25,470 --> 00:10:28,320
 Watch them.

92
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:29,320
 Stole my spade.

93
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:30,700
 No!

94
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:31,700
 He stole my pitchfork.

95
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:33,660
 He stole it and it was him.

96
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:34,660
 No, it was him.

97
00:10:34,660 --> 00:10:40,200
 You were up in my back.

98
00:10:40,200 --> 00:11:02,410
 Hold me.

99
00:11:02,410 --> 00:11:03,890
 Glad that you're here.

100
00:11:03,890 --> 00:11:05,140
 You took your time.

101
00:11:05,140 --> 00:11:06,110
 Calm down, young man.

102
00:11:06,110 --> 00:11:07,460
 There's no hurry.

103
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:08,900
 Are you up there?

104
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:09,900
 Uh-huh.

105
00:11:09,900 --> 00:11:37,660
 Young man.

106
00:11:37,660 --> 00:11:40,240
 Baby.

107
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:41,240
 Doing very well.

108
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:42,240
 Mother is too.

109
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:43,240
 It's a little loud.

110
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:59,500
 That's what you got.

111
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:00,760
 See the little jackdaw.

112
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:03,900
 Right.

113
00:12:03,900 --> 00:12:07,230
 It should be flying by now.

114
00:12:07,230 --> 00:12:08,380
 They're ready.

115
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:21,120
 You show them how.

116
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:50,760
 It's there, but they don't appear to be too worried.

117
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:53,700
 They don't realize it's an animal dangerous for them.

118
00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:54,800
 They have to be taught.

119
00:12:55,070 --> 00:12:55,680
 Oh, how?

120
00:12:55,950 --> 00:12:56,550
 Look there.

121
00:12:59,790 --> 00:13:07,720
 It's strange.

122
00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:10,680
 The jackdaws don't recognize their enemies instinctively, then.

123
00:13:11,070 --> 00:13:12,180
 No, they learn.

124
00:13:12,380 --> 00:13:13,580
 The parents have to teach them.

125
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:17,360
 But they attack instinctively when they think one of theirs is being attacked.

126
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:18,500
 Here, watch this.

127
00:13:24,150 --> 00:13:26,000
 Did you see?

128
00:13:26,220 --> 00:13:29,420
 The dark object I was holding in my hand, they thought that I was holding a bird.

129
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:30,380
 But now it's over.

130
00:13:30,570 --> 00:13:31,450
 They trust me again.

131
00:13:31,610 --> 00:13:31,930
 You see?

132
00:13:33,150 --> 00:13:34,890
 No, animals are not simple machines.

133
00:13:35,190 --> 00:13:36,310
 They can be sad or happy.

134
00:13:36,610 --> 00:13:38,650
 They're able to learn behavior and even love.

135
00:13:38,650 --> 00:13:41,990
 They may even develop a true affection for a person.

136
00:13:46,620 --> 00:13:49,600
 You see, Alfred, these small goslings have known me since birth

137
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:51,560
 and follow me as if I was their own mother.

138
00:13:54,060 --> 00:13:56,340
 You see, these ducks don't accept me.

139
00:13:57,050 --> 00:13:57,730
 Why's that?

140
00:13:58,040 --> 00:14:01,110
 I think it's because the mallards have their own special call.

141
00:14:01,170 --> 00:14:01,910
 I've observed that.

142
00:14:02,130 --> 00:14:04,510
 They have to hear it at birth if they're going to adopt anyone.

143
00:14:04,870 --> 00:14:05,910
 Here, let's give it a try. Come on.

144
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:30,600
 Why do they follow anymore?

145
00:14:31,030 --> 00:14:33,060
 It's because now you're too tall. Bend down.

146
00:14:41,170 --> 00:14:50,500
 He's such a nice man, too.

147
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:53,600
 The man imagines he's a duck, but I don't suppose he's dangerous.

148
00:14:53,820 --> 00:14:56,000
 You better watch him. You can never tell.

149
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:34,750
 It's incredible, not unlike mothers. They follow us around.

150
00:15:35,330 --> 00:15:59,540
 Ducks are tame. The geese, too. They're all mine.

151
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:00,780
 The jockdaws are, too.

152
00:16:00,920 --> 00:16:03,470
 Birds are here for everybody. Right to shoot them.

153
00:16:05,080 --> 00:16:08,300
 I wonder, would you pass me my scarf there?

154
00:16:19,690 --> 00:16:47,390
 He's hunted by the prey he hunts.

155
00:16:47,530 --> 00:16:50,280
 He's talking a lot of nonsense, all that ducks.

156
00:16:50,440 --> 00:16:52,140
 In my opinion, it's a lesson worth learning.

157
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:53,680
 A lesson, eh? What lesson?

158
00:16:53,940 --> 00:16:54,940
 You're on my prom.

159
00:16:57,580 --> 00:16:59,520
 Shotgun. Shotgun.

160
00:17:01,530 --> 00:17:04,480
 See, you encroach the National Guard, the Cavalry.

161
00:17:04,700 --> 00:17:07,160
 I'll sue you. You just wait. You're both...

162
00:17:07,160 --> 00:17:29,910
 These animals around, I'm worried about the baby.

163
00:17:29,990 --> 00:17:32,330
 I wonder if we put the animals in cages.

164
00:17:32,880 --> 00:17:34,620
 They go to their cages at night.

165
00:17:35,060 --> 00:17:36,360
 Have to feel they're free.

166
00:17:36,790 --> 00:17:38,200
 But you gave me a good idea.

167
00:17:42,690 --> 00:17:48,940
 Do you really think all the animals will go in?

168
00:17:49,240 --> 00:17:50,260
 No, they won't.

169
00:17:50,380 --> 00:17:51,280
 But we certainly can.

170
00:17:57,620 --> 00:18:04,860
 Turned upside down.

171
00:18:04,980 --> 00:18:06,500
 Animals are feeding the humans.

172
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:29,850
 You detangle my wool.

173
00:18:51,830 --> 00:19:03,360
 Say it, the Lorenz's are a little bit crazy.

174
00:19:08,830 --> 00:19:11,930
 Chimney Seep was an enemy.

175
00:19:12,170 --> 00:19:13,020
 He's going to be lost.

176
00:19:25,390 --> 00:19:34,010
 That's him.

177
00:19:43,470 --> 00:19:50,680
 He's drunk, he is.

178
00:19:59,380 --> 00:20:05,690
 Look, he's on his shoulders.

179
00:20:05,690 --> 00:20:09,020
 People have all kinds of wrong ideas about animals.

180
00:20:09,020 --> 00:20:10,240
 Take wolves.

181
00:20:10,240 --> 00:20:13,280
 How many times do you hear these animals are wild and cruel?

182
00:20:13,280 --> 00:20:15,360
 They're really quite different.

183
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:17,180
 They hunt only to survive.

184
00:20:17,180 --> 00:20:25,940
 Hunts are hungry.

185
00:20:25,940 --> 00:20:27,550
 We haven't eaten for three days.

186
00:20:27,550 --> 00:20:44,380
 All except the young ones.

187
00:20:44,380 --> 00:21:00,540
 The one we go for, but be careful.

188
00:21:00,540 --> 00:21:03,270
 Kill a wolf with one kick of their hooves.

189
00:21:03,270 --> 00:21:04,570
 Softly.

190
00:21:04,570 --> 00:22:04,290
 Same reason dogs, which as you know are related to wolves,

191
00:22:04,290 --> 00:22:06,010
 only very rarely kill each other.

192
00:22:06,010 --> 00:22:40,560
 I wouldn't say it's science, 'cause me, too.

193
00:22:40,560 --> 00:22:41,660
 I always look at the animals.

194
00:22:41,660 --> 00:22:44,570
 Just the other day, I saw a great cat, another one.

195
00:22:44,570 --> 00:22:47,550
 Just looking, watching, it takes far more.

196
00:22:47,830 --> 00:22:50,490
 You have to observe, compare, theorize.

197
00:22:50,730 --> 00:22:51,690
 Then it's a science.

198
00:22:52,030 --> 00:22:57,110
 You remember, Democritus studied a grain of sand and he broke it and deduced the idea of atoms.

199
00:22:57,540 --> 00:22:59,800
 Galileo observed the moon, the sun, and the planets.

200
00:23:00,420 --> 00:23:03,400
 The theorist, he figured out the movement of the heavenly bodies.

201
00:23:03,480 --> 00:23:05,220
 Newton, an apple, fell on his head.

202
00:23:06,070 --> 00:23:08,380
 He comes up with laws of gravitation.

203
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:10,220
 Faraday liked watching lightning.

204
00:23:10,680 --> 00:23:11,840
 And what was the result?

205
00:23:12,350 --> 00:23:13,790
 The laws of electromagnetism.

206
00:23:13,790 --> 00:23:16,870
 That is real science.

207
00:23:17,340 --> 00:23:19,380
 Lawrence, too, observed, in his case, animals.

208
00:23:19,480 --> 00:23:20,860
 He saw they had personalities.

209
00:23:21,360 --> 00:23:25,420
 And he devised certain laws that apply to all sorts of animal behavior patterns,

210
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:27,100
 even applicable to humans.

211
00:23:27,420 --> 00:23:30,820
 For instance, animals of the same species rarely kill each other.

212
00:23:32,210 --> 00:23:34,940
 You reject, like me.

213
00:23:34,940 --> 00:24:34,050
 Human beings, too, deep down, still remains of that primitive brain we inherited from the animals.

214
00:24:34,630 --> 00:24:38,460
 No doubt there is the command, you shall not kill your own species.

215
00:24:38,640 --> 00:24:40,660
 But when life becomes really too difficult,

216
00:24:41,090 --> 00:24:43,610
 when animals are confined to a limited environment,

217
00:24:44,240 --> 00:24:49,950
 There are too many of them, there's too little to live on, then their aggression takes over, with animals and with people.

218
00:24:49,950 --> 00:25:04,730
 Well, there's ambition, and there's pride, and, well, these feelings are unknown to the animals.

219
00:25:04,730 --> 00:25:17,090
 It's become too easy to kill, letting others do it for us, come up with good reasons for bad actions, killing, but not knowing who, anonymous, abstract.

220
00:25:17,090 --> 00:25:23,360
 As Conrad Lawrence has tried to teach us, we should perhaps return to the good sense of the animals.

221
00:25:47,230 --> 00:25:48,640
 It's a good thing.

